#
# H3C FMCS (FTTR M-OLT) FPGA driver
#
# The FMCS FPGA gives the H3C HM2004-DU its FTTR master-OLT function: it bridges
# PLOAM and OMCI between up to sixteen sub-ONUs and the SoC.  The driver
# publishes /dev/fmcs_mci, the molt_ploam and molt_omci net devices, and the
# genl_* event families that the vendor userspace stack talks to.
#
# Say Y to build it in, or M to build the fttr-fmcs module.

config FTTR_FMCS
	tristate "H3C FMCS FTTR M-OLT FPGA support"
	depends on ARCH_AIROHA || COMPILE_TEST
	depends on GPIOLIB
	select FW_LOADER
	help
	  Driver for the FMCS downlink FPGA on the H3C HM2004-DU FTTR gateway.
	  It exposes the vendor ioctl ABI on /dev/fmcs_mci, the molt_ploam and
	  molt_omci control net devices, and the genetlink event families used
	  by the stock PLOAM and OMCI stacks.

	  The FPGA is reached either through a SPI controller (when the device
	  tree node is a child of one) or by bit-banging GPIO lines named sck,
	  mosi, miso and cs.  On stock hardware the controller that carries the
	  FPGA is claimed by the SPI-NAND driver.  The physical GPIO transport
	  pins have not been identified on the HM2004-DU and must be validated
	  before use.

	  If unsure, say N.

config FTTR_FMCS_LOAD_AT_PROBE
	bool "Load the FPGA bitstream at probe time"
	depends on FTTR_FMCS
	default y
	help
	  Configure the FPGA from /lib/firmware/FTTR_TOP.sbit when the driver
	  probes, reproducing what the vendor fpga_load.ko module did at load
	  time.  The bitstream is shifted out of the configuration port MSB
	  first, byte for byte, exactly as shipped.

	  The /proc/fpgaLoad entry that reports the clock hold times and accepts
	  "load <t1> <t2> <ignored>" to retune them and reload is built either
	  way; this option only decides whether a load also happens on probe.

	  Disable this if a bootloader or another component already configures
	  the FPGA.
